id: decisions/012-shared-key-vocabulary
title: "One key vocabulary for permits, loops, colours"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'vocabulary-locality'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: vocabulary-locality
axis_gloss: "whether one vocabulary governs the whole corpus, or each record, folder or layer coins its own"
poles:
  a: per-record-local-vocabulary
  b: single-shared-vocabulary
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 23.5
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(28) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Should there be one shared set of key names for what an agent may and may not
  do, for where a record sits in the loop, and for what a colour means, or may
  each record coin its own?

why_it_matters: >
  Every authored rule on this axis says one grammar (six fixed loop relations,
  one relational grammar, exact palette values, one canonical operator), yet
  across 22 agent and automation files the permit key appears six ways and the
  deny key seven, and the chrome contract hardcodes hexes the brand contract
  told it to reference. Once answered, a bot that checks its own boundary can
  read one key instead of guessing among may/allowed/agent_may, and a child Yawn
  inherits a vocabulary rather than a naming habit.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    23 judgments sit at per-record-local-vocabulary (0 authored) and 5 at
    single-shared-vocabulary (5 authored). The repository asserts both directions
    on this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Which of the six permit spellings and seven deny spellings Dave wants kept;
    and whether the differing core/ section names (invariants, boundary, lock) are
    meant to signal different strengths or are drift.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Each record may name its permit, deny, boundary, loop-position and
      colour-role keys as it sees fit. The shared-vocabulary rules in
      core/movement-loop.yawn and interface/yawn-brand-v1.yawn apply only inside
      their own files.
    cost: "Nothing to migrate; but core/movement-loop.yawn projection_rule and interface/yawn-brand-v1.yawn rules must be narrowed to say they do not bind siblings."
    risk: "No bot can compute over boundaries across files; a policy checker has to be rewritten per contract."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None can enforce this; the record would be a scope note on the two rule files."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0039"
      - "J0050"
      - "J0132"
      - "J0404"
      - "J0465"
      - "J0467"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: One shared field vocabulary governs every .yawn record. The permit key,
      the deny key, the boundary section name, the loop-position key and the colour
      role names are each fixed in one named record, and every record in the corpus
      uses those spellings.
    cost: "Rewrite permit/deny keys in ~22 agent and automation files, unify section names (invariants/boundary/stable_claims/must_not/lock) across core/, fix examples/participation-choice.yawn loop key, re-point interface/yawn-chrome-v1.yawn to CSS variables."
    risk: "Section names may carry deliberate strength differences (lock vs boundary) that a forced merge erases."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ that walks every active .yawn and rejects any permit/deny/loop-position key outside the fixed set; extend tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs to reject hex literals in yawn-chrome-v1 that are not declared brand variables."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0089"
      - "J0151"
      - "J0457"
      - "J0466"
      - "J0479"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Keys a bot reads to decide what it may do or how to render are fixed in
      one shared vocabulary: the permit key, the deny key, the loop-position key,
      the colour role names, and the canonical operator. Prose section names
      (invariants, stable_claims, lock) stay free. The shared set is listed by name
      in one record.
    cost: "Rewrite permit/deny keys in agents/ and automation/, fix the loop key in examples/participation-choice.yawn, re-point yawn-chrome-v1 hexes; leave core/ section naming alone."
    risk: "The line between machine-read and prose keys is argued case by case."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ over agents/ and automation/ that rejects permit/deny keys outside the fixed set, and an extension to tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs that rejects undeclared hex literals in interface/yawn-chrome-v1.yawn."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0089"
      - "J0457"
      - "J0466"
      - "J0479"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.65
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    All five authored judgments sit on the shared-vocabulary side and all 23
    local-vocabulary judgments are revealed, so this is a written rule with a
    large unwritten exception. But the authored rules are each domain-scoped
    (loop, relational grammar, interface), and none says every .yawn key is
    shared. The one contract-defined rule with a test (J0466,
    tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs) is already violated by J0467 without the
    test noticing, which shows the rule needs a checker, not just a restatement. C
    fixes the keys a bot actually reads and leaves prose naming free, which is the
    smallest rule that would have caught J0039, J0050, J0404 and J0467.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "One record (agents/node.yawn or core/agency.yawn) lists the permit key, deny key and loop-position key by name, and a test in tests/ rejects records in agents/ and automation/ that use other spellings."
    - "tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs fails if interface/yawn-chrome-v1.yawn carries a hex literal not declared in interface/yawn-brand-v1.yawn css_variables, and passes after the chrome file is fixed."
    - "examples/participation-choice.yawn uses one loop-position key."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A new automation contract lands with a fresh permit key (for example
    permitted:) and npm test stays green.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0039"
  - "J0050"
  - "J0132"
  - "J0404"
  - "J0465"
  - "J0467"
  - "J0089"
  - "J0151"
  - "J0457"
  - "J0466"
  - "J0479"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
